WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.630
- All right, let's check
out this 3M cup mask.

00:00:02.630 --> 00:00:03.830
Is it legit or is it not?

00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:04.920
Let's find out.

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.870
- [Narrator] This is an N95 mask,

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:08.740
the same type the Biden administration

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:10.820
has begun distributing for free.

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:12.850
- Oh, the concentration's
coming way down here.

00:00:12.850 --> 00:00:14.668
So that's a really good sign.

00:00:14.668 --> 00:00:18.060
- [Narrator] N95 is a
type of respirator mask.

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:20.460
KN95 is another.

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:21.293
- And it's very comfortable.

00:00:21.293 --> 00:00:22.440
I have a good fit here.

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:24.450
- [Narrator] Properly
fitted, masks like these

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:25.750
can provide better protection

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:27.610
from the particles that carry COVID.

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:30.470
- If everybody was wearing
a respirator during a surge,

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:31.720
you would stun transmission

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:34.810
because you have two-way
protection approaching 95%.

00:00:34.810 --> 00:00:36.960
- [Narrator] That's 95%
of particles blocked

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:39.290
from being inhaled and exhaled too,

00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:41.390
thus the 95 in the names.

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:42.223
But that's only

00:00:42.223 --> 00:00:45.382
when these respirator
masks perform properly.

00:00:45.382 --> 00:00:47.060
- I wanna make sure I
get a good seal on it.

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:48.720
- [Narrator] Problem is it's tough to know

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:52.590
if your mask actually has
95% filtration efficiency.

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:55.390
A search on Amazon where masks
have been a top search term

00:00:55.390 --> 00:00:57.940
through the pandemic turns
up thousands of results.

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:00.784
And underperforming masks can creep in.

00:01:00.784 --> 00:01:02.450
- And notice in that
concentration coming down

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:04.865
but not as low as I'd like to see it.

00:01:04.865 --> 00:01:07.730
- [Narrator] So what can
you do to find a good mask

00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:10.423
and how easy is it to
accidentally get a bad one?

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:13.690
To find out, we bought some popular masks

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:16.370
on Amazon and put them
to the test with experts.

00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:18.040
- That's that difference
between there and there

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:20.540
is a sign of a good mask or a fake mask.

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:21.850
- [Narrator] This is Aaron Collins,

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:23.830
also known as the Mask Nerd.

00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:25.450
- So these are my collection of masks.

00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:27.620
I feel like I have every
mask that you might be able

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:28.860
to get from South Korea

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:30.510
or in the United States right now.

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:31.920
- [Narrator] Collins is
a mechanical engineer

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:33.760
with a background in aerosol science.

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:35.820
He says spotting poor-performing masks

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:38.050
is tough because they can look identical

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:39.120
to the good ones.

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:41.130
- That is the real crux of the problem

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:43.330
is you can have a good
mask or have a bad mask

00:01:43.330 --> 00:01:44.490
and you won't know.

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:45.650
- [Narrator] His solution?

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:47.430
Testing masks live on YouTube

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:50.440
from his home lab, which is his bathroom.

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:51.670
- If you just are given a mask

00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:52.910
and you don't know anything about masks,

00:01:52.910 --> 00:01:55.843
you're gonna think wow,
I've got 95% protection.

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:58.060
- [Narrator] So we asked Collins

00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:00.900
to do the same for us with the three N95s

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:04.100
and the five KN95s we ordered from Amazon.

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:07.580
All of the KN95s were
listed as top 10 bestsellers

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:09.730
and one of the N95s was labeled

00:02:09.730 --> 00:02:12.520
as an Amazon Choice at
the time of purchase.

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:15.240
The N95s we ordered all
received certification

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:16.290
from the National Institute

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:19.300
for Occupational Safety
and Health, or NIOSH.

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:20.980
We boxed up samples of the eight masks

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:23.230
and sent them off to Collins for testing.

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:24.063
- The test that I do

00:02:24.063 --> 00:02:26.580
is what's called a total
inward leakage test.

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:28.600
What you need for this type
of measurement is two things.

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:31.100
You need an aerosol source
and a particle counter.

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:33.470
- [Narrator] Without
getting too technical,

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:35.270
Collins uses these two machines

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:37.710
to test the filtration
efficiency of the masks

00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:39.920
to show how they perform as he wears them.

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:42.960
The results appear on
his laptop as he tests.

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:45.080
Basically the more this line falls,

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:48.160
the better the mask is at
filtering outside aerosols.

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:49.660
- One thing you'll notice right away

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:52.830
is that that concentration
has now dropped way down.

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:56.530
This indicates that it's very
likely a legitimate mask.

00:02:56.530 --> 00:02:58.070
- [Narrator] For his
test, Collins is looking

00:02:58.070 --> 00:03:00.350
for the highest filtration
percentage possible.

00:03:00.350 --> 00:03:03.480
He says N95s and KN95s typically reach

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:06.180
about 97% or higher.

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:07.920
Of the eight masks we sent him,

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:09.420
six performed well.

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:11.270
The one that was labeled Amazon Choice,

00:03:11.270 --> 00:03:14.930
this 3M N95 reached 99.7%.

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:18.240
- All right, well, 3M ones
that I got, definitely real.

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:19.840
- [Narrator] But two of the masks we sent

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:21.750
did not reach the filtration efficiency

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:23.560
Collins typically sees.

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:24.393
- Uh oh.

00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:27.520
Not looking good.

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:30.030
- [Narrator] This one,
a KN95 labeled Wanwane,

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:32.628
marked as an Amazon bestseller.

00:03:32.628 --> 00:03:33.820
- Yeah, 88%.

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:36.830
That there, my friends is most likely

00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:38.593
a very underperforming KN95.

00:03:40.270 --> 00:03:41.548
- [Narrator] And this one.

00:03:41.548 --> 00:03:42.381
- Undoubtful.

00:03:42.381 --> 00:03:45.240
- [Narrator] A NIOSH-approved
N95 from AERO PRO,

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:48.650
which came in at 92.5% in Collins' test.

00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:50.810
- I'm not convinced that
this is real either.

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:52.130
That's pretty low performing.

00:03:52.130 --> 00:03:53.600
- [Narrator] So after
getting these results,

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:55.890
the Journal sent both
underperforming masks,

00:03:55.890 --> 00:03:59.080
as well as the 3M N95 that tested well

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:02.760
to 4C Air, a lab that
performs the tests required

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:05.130
for masks to receive NIOSH approval.

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:07.200
Five samples of each mask were tested.

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:10.250
All five 3M masks tested
with a filtration efficiency

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:13.190
above 99%, which 4C Air said

00:04:13.190 --> 00:04:16.070
is in line with most N95s it tests

00:04:16.070 --> 00:04:20.090
but only one of the AERO PRO
masks met the 95% threshold.

00:04:20.090 --> 00:04:21.420
The rest fell short

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:25.200
with the mask averaging just
over 93% filtration efficiency.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:27.100
The lab said this indicates this batch

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:29.180
would likely not earn NIOSH approval

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:31.630
as none can fall below 95%.

00:04:31.630 --> 00:04:33.900
NIOSH said it can't comment on the results

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:36.730
as the testing was not done in its lab.

00:04:36.730 --> 00:04:40.170
All of the masks labeled
Wanwane vastly underperformed,

00:04:40.170 --> 00:04:44.150
averaging 81.9% with the
lowest filtration efficiency

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:46.570
at 77.5%.

00:04:46.570 --> 00:04:48.060
Based on these tests,

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:50.430
4C Air said this mask would fall short

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:52.490
of the KN95 standard.

00:04:52.490 --> 00:04:53.970
Both AERO PRO and the company

00:04:53.970 --> 00:04:55.840
that sells the Wanwane labeled masks

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:58.550
did not respond to multiple
requests for comment.

00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:00.017
In a statement, Amazon said,

00:05:00.017 --> 00:05:02.980
"We take credible allegations
of noncompliance seriously,

00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:04.700
conduct internal investigations,

00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:06.240
and hold bad actors accountable

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:08.910
for violating Amazon rules and policies."

00:05:08.910 --> 00:05:11.010
After The Journal reached
out to Amazon for comment,

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:13.410
the AERO PRO mask listing had been removed

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:15.950
and after The Journal shared
the test results with Amazon,

00:05:15.950 --> 00:05:18.850
the Wanwane mask was still
available for purchase.

00:05:18.850 --> 00:05:21.880
The white version was
listed as an Amazon Choice.

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:24.140
So are these masks fake?

00:05:24.140 --> 00:05:25.330
That's tough to say.

00:05:25.330 --> 00:05:27.420
They're still masks that filter particles

00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:30.460
but not at the 95% filtration
efficiency required

00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:33.265
by the N95 and KN95 standards

00:05:33.265 --> 00:05:36.750
and that leaves wearers less
protected than they think.

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:39.750
So what can you do if you have a bad mask?

00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:41.840
Well, you can try opening one up.

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:43.720
- Let's go ahead and take a look in here.

00:05:43.720 --> 00:05:46.580
So the filtration layer
is this layer here.

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:48.070
This is the melt blown

00:05:48.070 --> 00:05:50.980
electrostatically charged polymer layer,

00:05:50.980 --> 00:05:52.470
it's polypropylene.

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:54.030
- [Narrator] While the
exact number of layers

00:05:54.030 --> 00:05:55.800
in a respirator mask may differ,

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:58.240
the one to look for is this filter layer,

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:01.960
AKA the melt blown electrostatically
charged polymer layer.

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:03.130
- And you can see there's,

00:06:03.130 --> 00:06:04.550
you can sort of pull that apart

00:06:04.550 --> 00:06:06.390
and see the sort of intricate design

00:06:06.390 --> 00:06:07.730
that goes into a filter.

00:06:07.730 --> 00:06:08.700
- [Narrator] It's that filter

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:11.260
that traps the smallest aerosol particles,

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:13.520
the kind that can carry the virus.

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:15.060
But seeing the filter isn't enough

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:17.760
to know for sure that
it's a legit mask either.

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:20.170
The AERO PRO and the
Wanwane-labeled masks,

00:06:20.170 --> 00:06:22.670
for example, both had filtration.

00:06:22.670 --> 00:06:24.830
Collins says there are
other clues you can look for

00:06:24.830 --> 00:06:26.760
to spot a possibly poor mask,

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:28.940
starting with the labeling on the outside.

00:06:28.940 --> 00:06:31.970
- They've got most of the
KN95 standard requirements,

00:06:31.970 --> 00:06:35.340
although they did not get the
manufacturer name on there.

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:36.380
Make sure the mask is trying

00:06:36.380 --> 00:06:38.020
to at least meet some of
the standard requirement.

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:39.300
There are labeling requirements

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:40.940
as part of the KN95 standard.

00:06:40.940 --> 00:06:44.430
KN95s should say KN95.

00:06:44.430 --> 00:06:49.430
They should also say GB2626-2006 or 2019.

00:06:49.730 --> 00:06:52.560
Lastly, they should say
the manufacturer's name.

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:54.680
- If you just see the KN95 stamp on there,

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:55.513
you already know

00:06:55.513 --> 00:06:59.240
that that mask doesn't
meet the requirements

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:00.820
of the KN95 standard.

00:07:00.820 --> 00:07:02.560
So what else does it not meet?

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:05.250
- [Narrator] None of the
KN95 masks we tested,

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:07.160
even the high-performing ones had all

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:08.950
of this information on the mask,

00:07:08.950 --> 00:07:12.950
and that, Collins said, adds
to the confusion for consumers.

00:07:12.950 --> 00:07:14.170
The Centers for Disease Control

00:07:14.170 --> 00:07:15.400
and Prevention keeps a list

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:17.410
of known counterfeit KN95s

00:07:17.410 --> 00:07:19.880
or those misrepresenting NIOSH approval.

00:07:19.880 --> 00:07:21.560
- The N95 labeling requirements

00:07:21.560 --> 00:07:23.010
are much more stringent.

00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:23.970
- [Narrator] For these masks,

00:07:23.970 --> 00:07:26.460
they also need to say N95.

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:27.940
They need to have the NIOSH logo

00:07:27.940 --> 00:07:29.270
or text on there,

00:07:29.270 --> 00:07:31.130
as well as the approval number starting

00:07:31.130 --> 00:07:32.850
with T and C.

00:07:32.850 --> 00:07:35.640
They also must have the
manufacturer's name.

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:37.540
You can use the information
printed on the mask

00:07:37.540 --> 00:07:39.190
to verify its NIOSH approval

00:07:39.190 --> 00:07:41.330
against the list on the CDC's website.

00:07:41.330 --> 00:07:43.320
Still, that isn't foolproof either.

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:46.290
The AERO PRO N95 met these
labeling requirements

00:07:46.290 --> 00:07:49.340
and appeared on the NIOSH
approval list, for example.

00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:51.640
Here Collins has another tip.

00:07:51.640 --> 00:07:52.800
Check the supplier.

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:54.720
- If you're buying from a big box retailer

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:56.820
or you're buying from the
manufacturer's website

00:07:56.820 --> 00:07:58.350
or through their Amazon web source,

00:07:58.350 --> 00:07:59.820
if you go to like 3M's direct site,

00:07:59.820 --> 00:08:00.890
the probability of getting a fake

00:08:00.890 --> 00:08:02.360
I think is really much lower

00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:04.360
because of that NIOSH requirement

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:06.710
and the regulation that happens around it.

00:08:06.710 --> 00:08:08.180
- [Narrator] In the case
of the AERO PRO mask,

00:08:08.180 --> 00:08:09.690
the seller was AccuMed,

00:08:09.690 --> 00:08:10.800
the medical supply company

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:13.080
that also sells third-party masks.

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:14.640
In a statement, AccuMed said

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:17.227
that as a distributor of
NIOSH N95 respirators,

00:08:17.227 --> 00:08:19.440
"We place our trust in the CDC, NIOSH

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:21.760
and the FDA to validate
the manufacturers listed

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:23.250
on their websites as approved."

00:08:23.250 --> 00:08:24.540
AccuMed said it also plans

00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:26.970
to start independently
testing the masks it sells.

00:08:26.970 --> 00:08:28.980
It no longer sells the AERO PRO mask

00:08:28.980 --> 00:08:30.260
The Journal purchased.

00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:32.350
NIOSH said it will add the AERO PRO mask

00:08:32.350 --> 00:08:33.810
to the list of the products it plans

00:08:33.810 --> 00:08:35.160
to audit this year.

00:08:35.160 --> 00:08:36.220
- I think it gets much harder

00:08:36.220 --> 00:08:37.660
to be trusting of the brands

00:08:37.660 --> 00:08:40.670
without some piece of third-party evidence

00:08:40.670 --> 00:08:42.350
that the masks that
they're supplying is good

00:08:42.350 --> 00:08:43.940
and I don't see that on Amazon listings.

00:08:43.940 --> 00:08:45.240
I don't see a third party

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:46.220
that has tested the mask

00:08:46.220 --> 00:08:47.640
and said this is the results.

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:49.520
- [Narrator] Amazon said it
has a rigorous seller vetting

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:50.670
and product review process

00:08:50.670 --> 00:08:54.220
for N95 and KN95s that
involves comparing masks

00:08:54.220 --> 00:08:56.410
against the CDC's counterfeit mask list

00:08:56.410 --> 00:08:58.170
and the NIOSH approval list,

00:08:58.170 --> 00:08:59.870
as well as obtaining test results

00:08:59.870 --> 00:09:03.930
from an accredited lab
in China for KN95 masks.

00:09:03.930 --> 00:09:05.520
When it comes to third-party sellers,

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:07.750
Collins suggests Project N95,

00:09:07.750 --> 00:09:10.547
a non-profit that only
sells masks it has vetted

00:09:10.547 --> 00:09:12.610
or there's one other option.

00:09:12.610 --> 00:09:14.340
And that's not to shop at all.

00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:15.470
The Biden administration

00:09:15.470 --> 00:09:18.180
has started distributing 400 million N95s

00:09:18.180 --> 00:09:20.010
to pharmacies and community health centers

00:09:20.010 --> 00:09:21.340
across the country.

00:09:21.340 --> 00:09:22.850
The masks are free and limited

00:09:22.850 --> 00:09:24.040
to three per customer.

00:09:24.040 --> 00:09:27.110
- I'll be honest, I wish
this happened in January 2021

00:09:27.110 --> 00:09:28.330
but it's better late than never.

00:09:28.330 --> 00:09:30.390
We will deal with more variants,

00:09:30.390 --> 00:09:33.210
we'll deal with more
epidemic surges in the future

00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:35.920
and the bar has now been raised.

00:09:35.920 --> 00:09:37.860
The bar has been raised to N95 masks.

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:39.717
This is critically important.

00:09:39.717 --> 00:09:42.467
(dramatic music)

